    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling core, led by **James Wood** (78.42) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91), both of whom benefit significantly from a favorable matchup against J.T. Ginn and Zack Littell, respectively. **James Wood** is our anchor today; his elite xwOBA of .437 combined with a leadoff role against a vulnerable arm makes him the highest-projected producer on the roster. Similarly, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) and **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) represent strong plays, as both benefit from dynamic platoon advantages and favorable park factors that elevate their floors well above the 70-point threshold. **Corbin Carroll** remains a mandatory start at 70.40; while his stolen base potential is slightly dampened by May\u2019s efficiency at holding runners, his sheer talent profile keeps him locked in our top-tier grouping.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench today involves **Seiya Suzuki** (57.43), who missed the cut by the narrowest of margins. Despite his strong projection, **Seiya Suzuki** was edged out by **Edwin Arroyo** (57.89), whose score is inflated by a significant park factor boost and the volatility of facing an arm like Sugano. While **Seiya Suzuki** provides reliable output, the optimizer prioritized the potential for higher variance in run production provided by **Edwin Arroyo** and **Gabriel Moreno** (57.04). We are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Josh Naylor**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Henry Bolte**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **James Wood**. Should any of these players slide down the order or receive a surprise rest day, their scores will require immediate re-evaluation. With SB being our primary scarcity concern, we are closely tracking the delivery times of opposing pitchers, noting that the matchups for **Brice Turang** and **Corbin Carroll** are hindered slightly by pitchers with sub-1.30s slide-step tendencies.",
